Z||000024||Mach10||12-20-2001||04:26 PM||||Triad: Have you EVER been terminally addicted to something? Have worked with people that have? Education DOES NOT WORK. It's a chemical addiction. Your body craves it. Your brain craves it. YOU crave it.. Lots of people "quit" only to fall off the wagon. There are many success stories, true, but these are exceptional. It takes plenty of time. There's a reason why there are so many addicts. A little more compassion is in order, I think. Free, clean needles, while not an immediate solution, protects users (so maybe they live to build up the strength to quit?) and the rest of us, too. Stopping the spread of such destructive diseases is EVERYBODIES CONCERN. My mother contracted Hepatitis TWICE (Lucky it was hep-b, not C). Know why? She was an ER nurse. She's NEVER done drugs, she's never been a whore. She was helping people who may or may not have fit into above categories.<BR>I'm with ray on this. Education is NOT an effective way to deal with this. Face it. Those children will be experimenting wether the condoms are there or NOT. Would you rather that they were protected, or would you rather they die a horrible slow death because of ONE mistake? <P>As for the 3rd world countries? Education there? Well, you're gonna have to teach 'em to read. You COULD just let darwin sort things out... But that's a VERY dangerous proposition. Remember that the more infected people you have, the more viruses are proliferating... More chance for mutation. So what happens when HIV or HEP-C develops a mutation that gives it a solid impermeable casing like Anthrax? Wow! Airborne, and resistant to almost anything besides pure chlorine and caustic soda... Again, NOT smart.<P>Arguing about morality is counter-productive. Halt the spread ANY way possible. THEN start dealing with the people. ||||reg||10
Z||000025||84Bill||12-20-2001||04:32 PM||||<BR>Bummer about your mom, My friend has Hep b he is a Cop<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B><BR> Halt the spread ANY way possible. THEN start dealing with the people. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>We have plenty of spare nukes and lots of open places  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000026||TRiAD||12-21-2001||12:09 AM||||Mach, are you saying we should give out free needles and send prostitutes to a government sanctioned "retirement" community, like Ray is? I don't think so, but I may be giving you too much credit. <P>To argue that education is worthless is to throw away everything that seperates us from animals. You're saying these people are too far gone to even bother with. Are you really saying we should be apathetic about them? That is what Ray is saying. "Who gives a crap, throw money at them and hope they'll go away, you can't help them, they're too far gone". <P>And yes, I have worked directly with drug, sex and alcohol (actually a drug also) addicted people. Education was intended for people BEFORE they have the problem. There are other ways to deal with existing cases. <P>Supplying their addiction, or "buying" their time is NOT the way to do it. You create an instant "welfare community" for prostitutes and people who are having a hard time making a living will become prostitutes just to have uncle sam (ray?) supply all their needs. <P>You CAN'T just start handing needles out to 4th graders, since "they'll all become junkies anyway, they may as well have clean needles.". This is one small step down ray's slippery slope. <P>Think about it. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Mach10||12-21-2001||02:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>Mach, are you saying we should give out free needles and send prostitutes to a government sanctioned "retirement" community, like Ray is? I don't think so, but I may be giving you too much credit. <P>To argue that education is worthless is to throw away everything that seperates us from animals. You're saying these people are too far gone to even bother with. Are you really saying we should be apathetic about them? That is what Ray is saying. "Who gives a crap, throw money at them and hope they'll go away, you can't help them, they're too far gone". <P>And yes, I have worked directly with drug, sex and alcohol (actually a drug also) addicted people. Education was intended for people BEFORE they have the problem. There are other ways to deal with existing cases. <P>Supplying their addiction, or "buying" their time is NOT the way to do it. You create an instant "welfare community" for prostitutes and people who are having a hard time making a living will become prostitutes just to have uncle sam (ray?) supply all their needs. <P>You CAN'T just start handing needles out to 4th graders, since "they'll all become junkies anyway, they may as well have clean needles.". This is one small step down ray's slippery slope. <P>Think about it. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry, should have stated... Education is not a way to deal with the PRESENT problem. It's useful for people who haven't been touched yet.... It's how humans evolve, through education.<P>But for people who have already stepped in it? What CAN you do for them otherwise? Telling them to stop 'cos it's bad for them won't work. Educating about hep/aids/whatever doesn't work. Some already have it, and some will get it, period. Show them pics all you want. You've worked with them. they NEED their fix like we need food. It's really sad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>By SUPPLYING the needles, you keep the disease where it is. You stop the spread. That's the idea. Not so that they can live longer, but also so that they aren't as much of a danger to themselves, and to others.<P>I don't suggest you supply condoms to the Grade-4s because they MIGHT be sexually active... You give it to the grade 7s and 8s who statistically ARE becoming active. It's HAPPENING already, so why not protect them while you still can? OR, you could wait until grade 12, when they've already contracted everything...<P>As for prostitutes... Legalize, and REGULATE. Get them OFF the street, and into legal, and CLEAN brothels. Give licenses... Screen Johns. Prostituition isn't going away. EVER. So why not help those involved? Don't get me wrong. Johns are filthy ****ing pigs... But I feel sorry for the Prostitutes... Unfortunately, though, this only helps those that are in the "biz" out of choice, and does nothing to those forced into it, or with no way out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> It's a MESSY topic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>And yes, Booze is a drug, too... Made the distinction because society seems to. I'd get my ASS kicked if I walked into a bar and called everyone junkies  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Final notes: I am only a proponent of the Needle-program, and for putting condoms where the statistical average age of sexual activity lies. I don't like government-funded cop-outs (welfare blows, in it's current state). I'm not sure what the "retirement facility" is about. I can only assume that's where they drop the whores off to sleep at night... That's not a solution. That's a band-aid. They have a habit of falling off, you know...<P>||||reg||12
Z||000028||TRiAD||12-21-2001||10:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>...By SUPPLYING the needles, you keep the disease where it is. You stop the spread. That's the idea. Not so that they can live longer, but also so that they aren't as much of a danger to themselves, and to others.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>By supplying the needles, you supply a clean needle ONCE, and only to the person you give it to. Unless you're going to make him USE IT in front of you, and you dispose of it, it will still get passed around. Ray hadn't thought that far ahead, apparently.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>...I'd get my ASS kicked if I walked into a bar and called everyone junkies   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LOL!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Yes, you would. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Final notes: I am only a proponent of the Needle-program, and for putting condoms where the statistical average age of sexual activity lies. I don't like government-funded cop-outs (welfare blows, in it's current state).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My concern with the needles (other than potentially promoting their addiction further) has been stated. <BR>As for condoms, what about when more kids begin having sex at that age (it's less than 25% now) and the age of frequent sexual activity drops, because of the frequency at younger ages? Are you going to continually provide condoms to younger and younger kids because the age keeps dropping?<P>I guarantee you, many kids will view condom availability as condoning sexual activity at their age "they wouldn't give them to us if we weren't supposed to use them". <P>Also  why is it the SCHOOLS are expected to do this? Whatever happened to a PARENT raising their childen? If you want your daughter to have a pack of condoms in her book bag when she's 13, YOU give them to her privately. Don't make my daughter carry them as well. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||TRiAD||12-21-2001||10:59 AM||||Plus, if those 12 and 13 year old kids won't come to their parents for them, and they won't go to the store and get them, what makes you think they're going to go ask a school counselor or nurse for them?<P>The stigma is the same.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Mach10||12-21-2001||11:54 AM||||In order:  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>By supplying the needles, you supply a clean needle ONCE, and only to the person you give it to. Unless you're going to make him USE IT in front of you, and you dispose of it, it will still get passed around. Ray hadn't thought that far ahead, apparently. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>VERY good point... I still maintain that it's better than nothing. But yes, there are associated problems  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I see your concern with the school programs... It's a rock and a hard place. How do you protect them, while at the same time try to discourage? It's a confusing message, and can only add to the already raging fire of hormones, nihilism, and rebellion... Whereas I hope to some day have a daughter of my own (Looking forward to the "Evil Girlfriend's Dad" bit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">), society sure ain't makin' it easy... There is no "magic bullet" that will work. Only intesive social reform, and major changes can fix this. And we both know that it won't happen any time soon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000031||JSocha||12-21-2001||12:20 PM||||When I was in high school I used to be terrified of my girlfriend’s father, who I believe suspected me of wanting to place my hands on his daughter’s chest. He would open the door and immediately affect a good-naturedly murderous expression, holding out a handshake that, when gripped, felt like it could squeeze carbon into diamonds.<P>Now, years later, it is my turn to be the dad or in my case, step dad of two teenage girls and a 20 year old. Remembering how unfairly persecuted I felt when I would pick up my dates, I do my best to make my daughter’s suitors feel even worse. My motto: wilt them in the living room and they’ll stay wilted all night.<P>“So,” I’ll call out jovially. “I see you have your nose pierced. Is that because you’re stupid, or did you merely want to APPEAR stupid?”<P>You want education to see if it deters?<P>Well then, as a step dad, I have some basic rules, which I have carved into two stone tablets that I have on display in my living room.<P><B>Rule One</B> - If you pull into my driveway and honk you’d better be delivering a package, because you’re sure as heck not picking anything up.<P><B>Rule Two</B> - You do not touch my step daughter in front of me. You may glance at her, so long as you do not peer at anything below her neck. If you cannot keep your eyes or hands off of my daughter’s body, I will remove them.<P><B>Rule Three</B> - I am aware that it is considered fashionable for boys of your age to wear their trousers so loosely that they appear to be falling off their hips. Please don’t take this as an insult, but you and all of your friends are complete idiots. Still, I want to be fair and open minded about this issue, so I propose this compromise: You may come to the door with your underwear showing and your pants ten sizes too big, and I will not object. However, In order to assure that your clothes do not, in fact, come off during the course of your date with my daughter, I will take my electric staple gun and fasten your trousers securely in place around your waist.<P><B>Rule Four</B> - I’m sure you’ve been told that in today’s world, sex without utilizing a “barrier method” of some kind can kill you. Let me elaborate: when it comes to sex, I am the barrier, and I WILL kill you.<P><B>Rule Five</B> - In order for us to get to know each other, we should talk about Fieros, computers, politics, and other issues of the day. Please do not do this. The only information I require from you is an indication of when you expect to have my daughter safely back at my house, and the only word I need from you on this subject is “early.”<P><B>Rule Six</B> - I have no doubt you are a popular fellow, with many opportunities to date other girls. This is fine with me as long as it is okay with my step daughter. Otherwise, once you have gone out with my little girl, you will continue to date no one but her until she is finished with you. If you make her cry, I will make YOU cry.<P><B>Rule Seven</B> - As you stand in my front hallway, waiting for my daughter to appear, and more than an hour goes by, do not sigh and fidget. If you want to be on time for the movie, you should not be dating. My daughter is putting on her makeup, a process which can take longer than painting the Golden Gate Bridge. Instead of just standing there, why don’t you do something useful, like changing the oil in my Fiero?<P><B>Rule Eight</B> - The following places are not appropriate for a date with my daughter: Places where there are beds, sofas, or anything softer than a wooden stool. Places lacking parents, policemen, or nuns ( <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ). Places where there is darkness. Places where there is dancing, holding hands, or happiness. Places where the ambient temperature is warm enough to induce my daughter to wear shorts, tank tops, midriff T-shirts, or anything other than overalls, a sweater, and a goose down parka zipped up to her chin. Movies with a strong romantic or sexual theme are to be avoided; movies which feature chainsaws are okay. Hockey games are okay.<P>My daughter claims it embarrasses her to come downstairs and find me attempting to get her date to recite these eight simple rules from memory. I’d be embarrassed too—-there are only eight of them, for crying out loud! And, for the record, I did NOT suggest to one of these cretins that I’d have these rules tattooed on his arm if he couldn’t remember them. (I checked into it and the cost is prohibitive.) I merely told him that I thought writing the rules on his arm with a ball point might be inadequate-—ink washes off-—and that my wood burning set was probably a better alternative.<P>One time, when my wife caught me having one of my step daughter’s would-be suitors practice pulling into the driveway, get out of the car, and go up to knock on the front door (he had violated rule number one, so I figured he needed to run through the drill a few dozen times) she asked me why I was being so hard on the boy. “Don’t you remember being that age?” she challenged.<P>Of course I remember. Why do you think I came up with these eight simple rules?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000032||Patrick's Dad||12-21-2001||12:32 PM||||The only way to stop the spread of any non-airborne disease is quarrantine. That's essentially what wiped out the plague. The leeches didn't help much....<P>Unfortunately, in the current climate, it would be difficult both logistically and politically to quarrantine all the AIDS cases alone, much less the HIV cases.<P>And yes, I agree that the burden of education, be it moral or otherwise, is the responsibility of the parents. Despite what ray and others might think, I don't want the government to legislate morality, mine or anyone else's. I want the government to get the 773H out of the way when I tell my kids what's right and what's wrong. I don't want the schools to say what your parents/church/synagogue/mosque tells you is wrong. I don't want the school to supply my kids not only ideas that conflict with me, but items that contribute to the problems, especially when my money funds both of them (The ideas and the supplies).||||reg||1
